; Lifecycle tests of the protocol loop: the test client drives the built server
; for the replies, and a framed pipe drives it where only bytes will do (the
; exit codes and the frame reader's refusals). Both executables are
; dependencies, not libraries, because every test runs the real binary.
;
; Every input of these tests is either a `.t` file, which dune adds to the
; stanza itself, or one of the six entries below: the three helpers the tests
; run from the sandbox, the two executables they drive, and the initialize
; options one test reads from `test/fixtures`. A file a test reads that is not
; listed here would not invalidate its recorded result, so a fixture added
; later belongs in this list too.
;
; The binaries stay declared even though dune would build them anyway: the
; dependency is what makes a rebuilt server or client re-run the cram tests
; instead of leaving a recorded pass from the previous binary in place.
;
; `make test` removes the recorded results first and runs with the cache off,
; so every test here runs on every `make test`.

(cram
 (deps
  frames.sh
  bounded.sh
  record-pid.sh
  ../../fixtures/initialization-options.json
  %{exe:../../client/jide_test_client.exe}
  %{exe:../../../lsp-server/native/main.exe}))
